VDI18N.add('en', 'notes/binding-energy', [
"Nuclear Binding Energy: teaching notes",
"Nuclear Binding Energy",
"Modern physics; intro astronomy (stellar fusion)",
"The binding energy B is the energy needed to pull a nucleus apart into free protons and neutrons. It shows up as missing mass: B = [Z m(¹H) + N m_n − m(atom)]c², computed here from measured masses.",
"B/A rises steeply through the light nuclei, peaks at about {#} MeV near iron and nickel, and falls slowly to {#} MeV for uranium. Any reaction that moves nucleons toward the peak releases energy: fusion from the left, fission from the right.",
"The energy released, Q, equals the binding energy gained (when the number of protons and neutrons is unchanged). A change of about {#} MeV per nucleon is a million times the energy per atom of chemistry.",
"The liquid drop model explains the shape: every nucleon binds to its neighbors (volume), minus losses for nucleons at the surface, for proton repulsion, and for an excess of neutrons over protons.",
"Nuclei with {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#} or {#} protons or neutrons are bound more tightly than the smooth model predicts: closed shells.",
"Students should know E = mc², {#} u = {#} MeV/c², and Z, N and A. The demo uses mass excesses Δ = (M − A u)c², so B = ZΔ(¹H) + NΔ(n) − Δ(A, Z). Views: <strong>Curve</strong>, <strong>Liquid drop</strong>, <strong>Shell effects</strong> and <strong>Chart</strong>. Click a dot to select a nuclide; the <strong>Mass number A</strong> slider picks the most tightly bound nuclide of that A.",
"<strong>The curve of binding energy.</strong> Ask Q1 first. Each dot is a measured nuclide, blue if stable or found in nature. Click ⁴He, ¹²C and ¹⁶O: they sit above their neighbors. Point out the peak at ⁶²Ni ({#} MeV) and the slow fall to uranium.",
"<strong>Fusion: D + T.</strong> The reaction arrow runs from two loosely bound nuclei up to ⁴He. Read <strong>Q from the masses</strong> ({#} MeV) and <strong>Energy per kg of fuel</strong> (Q2, Q3).",
"<strong>Fission of uranium {#}.</strong> The arrow runs from uranium up to the fragments. Q is {#} MeV at once, about ten times the D + T value per reaction, but less per kilogram (Q4).",
"<strong>The liquid drop model.</strong> Start with only <strong>Volume</strong> checked, then add <strong>Surface</strong>, <strong>Coulomb</strong>, <strong>Asymmetry</strong> and <strong>Pairing</strong> one at a time, and watch the model curve take the shape of the data. Ask the prediction in Q5 before removing the Coulomb term. <strong>Fit to AME2020</strong> shows how good five numbers can be.",
"<strong>Why iron is the end of the line.</strong> Near the peak, compare <strong>B/A measured</strong> and <strong>Mass per nucleon M/A</strong> for ⁶²Ni, ⁵⁸Fe and ⁵⁶Fe: the order is not the same (Q6).",
"<strong>Magic numbers.</strong> Measured B minus the fitted drop, against N, then <strong>Against Z</strong>. The spikes sit at the magic numbers; ²⁰⁸Pb is doubly magic (Q7).",
"<strong>Build.</strong> Start from ¹¹C and add or remove protons and neutrons one at a time: the chart around it shows which nuclei are stable (black or white), which β⁻ or β⁺ decay, which α decay, and which do not hold together at all. <strong>Let it decay</strong> follows the main decay mode; from ²³⁸U the arrows trace the uranium series down to ²⁰⁶Pb. Read the last neutron's separation energy S<sub>n</sub> as you add neutrons: it drops toward zero at the drip line.",
"<strong>Q1.</strong> Predict: B/A for ²H is {#} MeV, for ⁴He {#} MeV, for ⁵⁶Fe {#} MeV and for ²³⁵U {#} MeV. Which of these releases energy: fusing two light nuclei, splitting a uranium nucleus, fusing two iron nuclei, splitting an iron nucleus? Explain using the shape of the curve.",
"<strong>Q2.</strong> The mass excesses are Δ(¹H) = {#} MeV, Δ(n) = {#} MeV and Δ(⁴He) = {#} MeV. Find the binding energy of ⁴He and its B/A. What fraction of the mass of two hydrogen atoms and two neutrons disappears when they form one ⁴He atom? Check with the demo.",
"<strong>Q3.</strong> For D + T → ⁴He + n, the mass excesses are Δ(²H) = {#} MeV and Δ(³H) = {#} MeV (with the values in Q2). Find Q. The deuterium and tritium are nearly at rest; how is Q shared between the neutron and the alpha particle, and why?",
"<strong>Q4.</strong> Uranium sits at {#} MeV per nucleon, and the fragments ¹⁴¹Ba and ⁹²Kr at about {#} and {#} MeV. Estimate the energy released in ²³⁵U + n → ¹⁴¹Ba + ⁹²Kr + 3n from these numbers. Why does fission release about ten times more energy per reaction than D + T, but D + T about five times more per kilogram of fuel?",
"<strong>Q5.</strong> Predict, then check in <strong>Liquid drop</strong>: if protons did not repel each other (turn off the <strong>Coulomb</strong> term), what would the model curve of B/A do at large A? What does your answer say about why heavy nuclei can fission at all?",
"<strong>Q6.</strong> ⁶²Ni has the highest binding energy per nucleon, yet ⁵⁶Fe has the lowest mass per nucleon of any nucleus. Explain how both can be true. Use the <strong>Mass per nucleon M/A</strong> readout for both.",
"<strong>Q7.</strong> In <strong>Magic numbers</strong>, ²⁰⁸Pb (Z = {#}, N = {#}) is bound about {#} MeV more tightly than the fitted liquid drop. What does it mean for a nucleus to have a closed shell, and what is the chemical analogy? Find one more nuclide on the plot with an unusually large positive value and check whether its Z or N is magic.",
"<strong>\"Binding energy is energy stored in the nucleus that comes out in reactions.\"</strong> It is energy the nucleus does not have: the energy you must supply to take it apart. A more tightly bound nucleus has less mass, and the difference is what is released.",
"<strong>\"Iron releases the most energy.\"</strong> Iron is the end point: nothing is gained by fusing or splitting nuclei near the peak.",
"<strong>\"Fission releases energy because uranium is unstable.\"</strong> It releases energy because the fragments are bound about {#} MeV per nucleon more tightly.",
"Masses: AME2020 ground state mass excesses ({#} nuclides with measured masses; values estimated from systematics are left out). B = ZΔ(¹H) + NΔ(n) − Δ(A, Z). Atomic masses count the electrons with the protons, so the electrons' own binding (at most {#} MeV for uranium, {#}%) is left in B.",
"Q = Σ Δ(before) − Σ Δ(after) for nuclei at rest in their ground states. For D + T, fission and triple alpha, Q equals the binding energy gained. For the pp chain, two protons become neutrons, so Q is less than the binding of ⁴He by {#}(m_n − m_H)c² = {#} MeV.",
"Fission uses one fragment pair; real fission gives a spread of pairs, and later beta decays bring the total to about {#} MeV.",
"Liquid drop: B = a_V A − a_S A^({#}/{#}) − a_C Z(Z−{#})/A^({#}/{#}) − a_A(N−Z)²/A ± a_P/A^({#}/{#}), with the textbook values a_V = {#}, a_S = {#}, a_C = {#}, a_A = {#}, a_P = {#} MeV, or a least squares fit to every measured nucleus with A ≥ {#}. The model curve follows its own valley of stability Z(A) and leaves out pairing; it is poor for light nuclei (dashed below A = {#}).",
"Homework: from the masses, Q for {#} ¹H → ⁴He is {#} MeV but the binding energy of ⁴He is {#} MeV. Explain the difference, then estimate how many kilograms of hydrogen the Sun turns into helium each second from its luminosity, {#} × {#}²⁶ W."
]);
